   X-Folks,

   As promised, here are excerpts from the DMS-III-R regarding
   antisocial personality disorder:

       The essential feature of this disorder is a pattern of
       irresponsible and antisocial behavior beginning in
       childhood or early adolescence and continuing into
       adulthood.  For this diagnosis to be given, the person
       must be at least 18 years of age and have a history of
       Conduct Disorder before the age of 15. . . .

   Following, are the diagnostic criteria for antisocial personality
   disorder.  (Just for the fun of it, let's keep count of how many
   of the criteria apply to Tim May):

   [criteria elided]

This makes it abundantly clear that "antisocial personality
disorder" is not a medical condition,  instead it is a measure
of how well a person fits sometimes arbitrary expectations of
a generally sheltered segment of academia.  Not only do the
criteria show that there is no medical knowledge implicit in
such a diagnosis, but there is a complete lack of any medical
criteria, other than the exclusion of "manic episodes" and
"schizophrenia".  This is legalistic mumbo-jumbo, not science.

The biggest flaw in the whole thing is a complete lack of
taking the person's environment into account.  For example,
some unfortunate people live in situations where defending
themselves with a weapon is a necessity (part B, section 4),
which is a property of their environment, not their personality.

It is also clear that you would have to know Tim May fairly
well, or at least interview people who do, to make a diagnosis
on him.

In general, I think the mind-set implicit in creating such a
set of criteria is probably more dangerous than the average
person who meet them.

Andy

