From: "Kathleen Cadmus" <kcadmus@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: No More Prisons

In Message Tue, 4 Jan 94 21:04:05 EST, Andy Wilson <ajw@Think.COM> writes:


>I disagree with one of your premises,  the deploring of all coercive acts.
>
>I don't claim to deplore all coercive acts.  Coercive acts made in
>self-defense, or the defense of others or property, are justified.
>I have committed such acts in the past and will in the future when
>necessary.
>

I agree with Andy (I think) that the critical issue here is when an adherent
to extropian principles will nonetheless practice coercion. There are (at
least) the following four options:

    1) Never practice coercion
    2) Practice coercion only to thwart an immediate threat of coercion or
       to extract restitution from offenders.
    3) Also practice coercion preemptively to thwart probable future
       coercions.
    4) Practice coercion whenever we please.

I admit to having sympathy for all four of these choices! Suppose these four
choices are the law in protection agencies PA1 .. PA4, respectively.  PA1 is
incapable of enforcing its own law without breaking it. PA4 has nothing to
enforce! PA3 may be inherently unstable because of the unending escalation
caused by two agents mutually thwarting each others threats. Cold wars waste
incredible resources.

So I would still choose PA2. I would expect its services to be cheaper than
those of PA3. It is (arguably) simpler and requires no prison maintenance.
It would be the morality -- whoops!, I mean aesthetics -- that I would
encourage others to adopt as being most likely to lead to a libertarian
environment conducive to extropian pursuits.
