I suspect that "nigra" is the way that speakers of some Southern dialects 
pronounce "negro".  "Negro" is a phonologically unusual word in having two 
stressed syllables (one primary stress, one secondary) adjacent to each other; it 
is more typical for such syllables to be separated by a unstressed syllable (as 
in "battery").  Also, cf. Ohioans' pronunciation of "Ohio", with a schwa instead 
of an /o/ at the end.

